# Grafana frontend — quick agent guide

The canonical deep reference is the repo root [AGENTS.md](AGENTS.md) (commands, architecture, testing). This rule is a **short orientation** so the agent reaches for the right libraries and folders first.

## Stack (typical UI code)

| Area | Choice |
|------|--------|
| UI | React function components + hooks |
| Language | TypeScript |
| Styling | Emotion — prefer `useStyles2` from `@grafana/ui` (match surrounding files) |
| State / async | Redux Toolkit slices; RTK Query for API/data |
| Design system | `@grafana/ui` components and tokens — avoid one-off styling when a component exists |
| Data / models | `@grafana/data`, `@grafana/schema` (CUE-generated types where applicable) |
| Runtime / config | `@grafana/runtime` (services, backend settings access patterns in-repo) |
| Dashboard scenes | `@grafana/scenes` when working in scenes-based flows |
| Tests | React Testing Library; run `yarn jest --no-watch <path>` or `yarn test` with `--watchAll=false` for one-shot |

## Where code lives

All paths below are **relative to the Grafana repository root** (same directory as `package.json` and `pkg/`, not inside `.cursor/`).

- `public/app/` — SPA root; bundle entry starts at `public/app/index.ts` (bootstraps into `initApp`, etc.)
- `public/app/features/` — feature areas (dashboard, explore, alerting, etc.)
- `public/app/core/` — shared app services, utilities, cross-cutting UI
- `public/app/api/` — generated HTTP API clients used by the app
- `public/app/plugins/` — built-in plugins (many are Yarn workspaces)
- `packages/` — shared npm workspaces: `@grafana/ui`, `@grafana/data`, `@grafana/runtime`, etc.

## Conventions

- Follow **existing patterns in the file and sibling components** (imports, hooks, error handling, i18n).
- New user-visible strings: use the project i18n flow (see `make i18n-extract` in AGENTS.md); locale JSON under `public/locales/`.
- Security: avoid unsafe HTML; sanitize or use safe components when rendering untrusted content.

When in doubt, search the codebase for a similar feature and mirror its structure.
